nodes.body:
@xijiaat agreed. b538d076a restores the `check_remote_metadata` probe from #55471 as the first trigger, so confirmed missing metadata releases the peer after one failed read again, and the #55471 tests are back unchanged. The failed-request count is now a separate second trigger for peers whose metadata stays loaded, reusing the same drain, cleanup, backoff and re-handshake path. The branch is also rebased on main.

@NickLucche on the threshold, some data from our GLM-5.3 P/D deployment (H100, UCX backend):
- It is tied to NIXL 1.5.0 here. On 1.5.0 we saw failed pulls at about 0.5 incidents/min fleetwide; after going back to NIXL 1.3.2 we saw 0 failed pulls in 12 h over about 9.4M transfers. So you not seeing it on prod is consistent with this.
- On 1.5.0 we did not see isolated failures that recovered on their own. In a 96 min window, 199 of 238 failed reads were posted on an endpoint that was already `FAILED`, and before the fix one decode group failed 101 requests against two prefill workers within 47 min.

So I think the evidence points at dead connections more than transient errors, and I agree it should not become a knob. Two options: keep 2, which costs one extra failed request per incident, or release on the first failed read even with metadata loaded, which drops `_PEER_FAILED_REQS_BEFORE_RELEASE` entirely. @snadampal would first-failure work for you given the above? I can switch it in this PR either way.

